# Mechanical backstop for the "no credential in the repo" red line (minimal safety net; harness check comes with #50)
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*key*.json
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
*.p12
*.pfx
credentials*
secrets*
token*.txt

# ── Three mount points: repos / skills / knowledge ────────────────
# Teams git clone their business repos, existing skill libraries, and existing
# knowledge bases in to mount them; each keeps its own .git and version. The
# framework is read-only and non-locking, and does not track mounted content.
# Each mount point keeps only its own README.md (what to put / how to mount).
/repos/*
!/repos/README.md
# Everything under repos/ — files and sub-folders at any depth — stays untracked (only the
# mount-point README above is kept): a mounted repo's own agent entries (CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md)
# are that repo's asset, versioned in its own git, and must never enter the framework git.
# Local-only mirror of which repos are actually mounted (see workspace.yaml's frozen `repositories: []`
# and scripts/setup-repos) — same "local, never tracked" idea as /repos/* above, one layer up.
/workspace.local.yaml
# Atomic-write temp file (setup-repos / setup-skills write `workspace.local.yaml.tmp` next to the
# sidecar, then os.replace it). An interrupted run leaves it behind; ignore it so a broad
# `git add -A` can never commit the local sidecar data the atomic write exists to protect.
/workspace.local.yaml.tmp
/skills/*
!/skills/README.md
# Template-bundled skills: written against the template itself, tracked and evolved in this repo
# via normal PR flow (they have no separate upstream). Everything else under skills/ stays mounted —
# including team-native skills, which live in team-context/skills/<id>/ and reach skills/ as symlinks.
!/skills/workspace-context-bootstrap/
!/skills/workspace-onboarding/
!/skills/spec-authoring/
!/skills/token-usage-analysis/
!/skills/ai-usage-weekly/
!/skills/incident-fix/
!/skills/sre-autofix/
!/skills/grafana-sls/
!/skills/sre-inspect/
!/skills/security-patch/
!/skills/skills-source-loader/
!/skills/workspace-upstream-sync/
!/skills/workspace-update/
!/skills/code-map-query/
!/skills/code-map-scope/
/knowledge/*
!/knowledge/README.md
# knowledge/ is no longer a pure "clone-in" mount like repos/ and skills/ above: its content is
# authored/curated directly in this repo (there is no separate upstream to defer to), so it is
# framework-tracked like the template-bundled skills exception above, not gitignored wholesale.
!/knowledge/maps
!/knowledge/product
!/knowledge/workflows
!/knowledge/bug-fixes
!/knowledge/assets

# Git worktrees of a mounted repos/<repo> (e.g. for reviewing a PR in isolation): same "mounted
# business code, never enters the framework git" rule as /repos/* above, one layer up.
/worktrees/

# Skill-discovery symlinks (.claude/skills, .agents/skills): tracked as pointers only; the /skills/*
# rule above keeps the mounted content itself untracked. Mechanism: agent-instructions/mount-points.md.

# Golden-pack replay scaffolding (#44) — kept INSIDE each pack's own directory so repos/ stays
# reserved for real business mounts: .origin/ is a real git repo materialized from the tracked
# seed/ (stands in for upstream), .mount/ is the clone the recorded loop runs in. Never committed.
/platform-internals/golden/*/.origin/
/platform-internals/golden/*/.mount/
# Legacy location of the same scaffolding (pre-move); keep ignored so a stale materialization on an
# older checkout can never be committed by a broad `git add`. Delete the directory, then this line.
/.golden-origin/

# Findings — local, ad hoc analysis/review output the agent writes during a task (PR review notes,
# guides, checklists); ephemeral and per-task, not committed. Directory itself stays tracked (via
# its README) so a fresh checkout has somewhere to write findings to — same "keep dir, ignore
# contents" pattern as /repos/* above, but this isn't a mount point (no cloned/mounted content).
/findings/*
!/findings/README.md

# Run records (#50): local per-operator audit trail (may hold local paths); not committed.
# The directory is created by `ws` / harness_guard on first use — it is not part of the checkout,
# so a fresh clone has no empty runs/ to explain (#93). Format spec and a scrubbed demo specimen
# are tracked as platform-internals/runs-format.md and platform-internals/runs-example-harness-block.jsonl.
/runs/

# Common local noise
.DS_Store
__pycache__/
node_modules/
*.log

# Per-machine MCP server config written by `suites/sre-inspect/run mcp-config --write` (points at the
# operator's docker + env; committing it would prompt every clone to trust a server).
/.mcp.json

# Operator's own copy of a suite's config (suites/<id>/config.yaml is the tracked template; the
# local copy wins when present so `git pull` never conflicts with filled-in repo ids / checks).
/suites/*/config.local.yaml
# Same idea for suites/sre-inspect: the department instance tracks tenant.yaml / routes.yaml, while a
# template checkout pointed at a real Grafana uses the gitignored *.local.yaml twins.
/suites/*/*.local.yaml
# suites/sre-inspect local HolmesGPT state (operator's config copy + spilled tool results)
/suites/sre-inspect/holmes/.holmes/
/suites/sre-inspect/holmes/tool-results/*
!/suites/sre-inspect/holmes/tool-results/.gitkeep

*.un~
*.swp
